<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Minoan civilisation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3000–1100 BCE</span> · Also known as Minoans, Minoan civilization</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Minoan civilisation was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> civilisation on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, named by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-evans">Arthur Evans</a> after the legendary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">King Minos</a>. From about 2000 BCE it built great palace complexes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaistos">Phaistos</a>, Malia and Zakros, used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a> script and traded widely across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. After about 1450 BCE Crete came under Mycenaean Greek rule, and the palaces were destroyed or abandoned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minoan-civilisation.jpg" alt="Minoan civilisation" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Sauber · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AMI_-_Oktopusvase.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Minoan civilisation was centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, with further settlements around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, and is often regarded as the first civilisation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It developed from the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> culture, whose oldest farming remains date to about 7000 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-minoan-period">Early Minoan</a> society shifted gradually from clan-based villages towards a more urban and stratified society. Crete lies on the sea routes between Europe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and is poor in metals, which is believed to have encouraged overseas trade.</p><p>The name is a modern coinage from the mythical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">King Minos</a>, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> believed ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>, and was popularised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-evans">Arthur Evans</a>. The people's own name for themselves is unknown, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> called them the Keftiu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first palaces were built in the Middle Minoan period, in the decades after about 1925 BCE, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaistos">Phaistos</a> and Malia, on sites used for communal ceremonies since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. Cretan hieroglyphic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a> developed in the following period, which ended in mass destructions usually attributed to earthquakes. Knossos, the largest site, had an estimated 18,000 people around 2000 BCE. The Late Minoan period produced many of the best-known objects, such as the snake goddess figurines and the La Parisienne fresco.</p><p>After about 1450 BCE the Minoans came under the cultural and perhaps political domination of the Mycenaean Greeks, forming a hybrid culture that lasted until about 1100 BCE. The civilisation was largely forgotten after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse">Late Bronze Age collapse</a> and was rediscovered in the early twentieth century through excavation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Little is known of Minoan society, and it is unclear whether there was ever a unified state. The buildings Evans called palaces are now thought to have served religious and economic purposes more than as royal residences. Minoan art shows no recognisable kings, and before about 1450 BCE a group of elite families may have controlled government and religion. Religion centred on peak sanctuaries and sacred caves, with symbols such as the bull, the double axe and the serpent.</p><p>The Minoans raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, sheep, pigs and goats, grew wheat, barley and chickpeas, and cultivated grapes, figs and olives. They traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Neither <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a> nor Cretan hieroglyphic has been deciphered, so the Minoan language is unknown.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Minoan civilization” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its Bronze Age palace, the largest of <b>Minoan Crete</b>, was destroyed in the fourteenth or thirteenth century BCE (the date of its final destruction is still debated), but the site was never abandoned.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Minoans</b> of Crete, in the 2nd millennium BCE, worshipped goddesses in palaces, caves and on mountain peaks, with symbols such as the double axe and the bull.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>By the end of the Late Cycladic sequence, around 1000 BC, the Cycladic and <b>Minoan civilisations</b> had essentially converged, and Naxos by then held a Mycenaean town.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cycladic-culture">Cycladic culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>A study of ancient DNA published in 2017 found the Mycenaeans genetically close to the <b>Minoans</b> of Crete, but with a share of ancestry related to peoples of the Eurasian steppe or Armenia that the Minoans lacked, which fits, though it does not prove, an arrival of Indo-European…</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Knossos was the centre of <b>Minoan civilisation</b>, with frescoes such as the bull-leaping scene, light wells, storerooms of pithoi and a theatral area.</blockquote><a href="/state/palace-state-of-knossos">Palace State of Knossos</a></li></ul></section>
